WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.440
- Hi, everyone. My name's Daniel Botsman.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.843
I teach Japanese history here at Yale,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.740
and it is an incredible privilege today

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.310
to be able to welcome to
campus Ms. Toshiko Tanaka

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.490
and her daughter, Ms. Reiko Tashiro.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.910
In addition to thanking
them both for being here

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.590
and for making the long,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.910
exhausting journey from
Japan to the East Coast,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.730
I do also want to
express my deep gratitude

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.120
to Anthony Shimamoto from
the University of Connecticut

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.560
School of Law, and Takeshi
Watanabe from Wesleyan University

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.290
for arranging their visit and opening up

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.743
this extraordinary opportunity
to us here at Yale.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.650
As I'm sure you all know,
Tanaka-san brings today

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.800
a message of the greatest importance,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.480
one that is hard to
believe anyone in the world

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.243
after 1945 could ever forget.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.500
And yet, according to the
Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.880
we now live in a time
of unprecedented danger

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.430
with the so-called Doomsday Clock set

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.340
at just 90 seconds to midnight,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.400
the closest we've ever
been, in other words,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.080
to true global catastrophe.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.470
And of course, all over the world,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.893
we've seen the continued
proliferation of nuclear weapons.

00:01:29.997 --> 00:01:31.860
As those of you who attended

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.020
last week's John W. Hall lecture

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.760
by the eminent political scientist,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.760
Richard Samuels from MIT
know, today, even in Japan,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.720
the only country in the world

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.290
to have experienced the
horrors of a nuclear attack,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.350
politicians are openly
discussing the acquisition

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.070
of nuclear weapons in order, they say,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.893
to counter the threats
of North Korea and China.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.510
Here at Yale, I'm glad to say
that there have been those

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.200
over the years who have written
and spoken about the horrors

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.880
that befell the people of Hiroshima

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.373
and Nagasaki in August, 1945.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.800
To give just two prominent
examples, John Hersey,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.540
who served as head of Pierson
College from 1965 to 1970

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.617
and taught writing here
for almost 18 years

00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.240
may have done more than any other American

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.060
to make ordinary people
in this country aware

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.280
of the horrors of the nuclear bombings

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.877
with his landmark 1946 essay, "Hiroshima."

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.270
50 years later in 1996, John
Treat, only recently retired

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:43.500
from the East Asian Languages
and Literatures Department

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.920
published his important
book, "Writing Ground Zero:

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.527
Japanese Literature and the Atomic Bomb."

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.800
It's so important that we have
access to works like these

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.264
that describe on the page what happened,

00:02:59.264 --> 00:03:03.540
but it's one thing to be able
to read about the horror.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.810
It is quite another to
be able to hear directly

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.523
from someone who lived through it.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.210
In this regard, I can only say again

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.030
what a truly special
privilege it is for us

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.290
to have Tanaka Toshiko-san
here with us today.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.660
Please join with me now in extending her

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.706
the very warmest possible welcome.

00:03:24.706 --> 00:03:26.057
(audience applauding)

00:03:26.057 --> 00:03:30.307
(Reiko Tashiro speaks in Japanese)

00:03:38.408 --> 00:03:41.160
- First of all, my mother
and I would really thank you

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.261
for having us here today.

00:03:43.261 --> 00:03:47.036
We would especially like to
extend our sincere gratitude

00:03:47.036 --> 00:03:48.930
to the people and organizations

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.613
who made this event possible.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.830
My name is Reiko Tashiro. I'm from Japan.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:00.180
I'm a second-generation
hibakusha or a A-bomb survivor.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.130
Since I was a child, I have
believed that nuclear weapons

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.263
cannot coexist with humanity.

00:04:08.263 --> 00:04:12.630
My mother once told me
that when I was born,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:16.860
the first thing my father had
to do was count my fingers

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.860
and toes to make sure that
I had no birth defect.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.070
In those days, even though
people survived themselves,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:30.393
they had to worry about radiation
damage to their children.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.550
Today, I would first give a
brief overview of the damage

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.520
caused by the atomic bomb on Hiroshima.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.360
Then my mother, Toshiko,
will tell you her story,

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.170
and we will have a question
and answer session afterward.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.290
During the talk, we would like
to share some of the photos

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.050
of Toshiko's enamel mural works,

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.680
which she has created for over 50 years,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.543
putting her wishes for peace.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.870
We would like to give you a prior warning.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.705
Some of the pictures
and stories being shared

00:05:09.705 --> 00:05:13.740
might cause some of you
discomfort and you might decide

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.723
whether you would prefer to avoid them.

00:05:21.257 --> 00:05:22.581
Oops, yes.

00:05:22.581 --> 00:05:24.990
It's not skipped. Yes.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.990
- On August 6th, 1945 at 8:15
AM the atomic bomb was dropped

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.310
targeting a bridge at the
center of Hiroshima city.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:40.170
The bomb exploded 2,000
feet above the ground,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:45.170
releasing intense heat,
blast, and radiation.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.910
Near the hypocenter, the blast is believed

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.233
to have had a speed of
about 1,000 feet per second.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.100
Buildings which were mostly
made of wood at the time

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.920
collapsed over a wide area.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.613
People were blown away
by the tremendous blast.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.080
At the moment of explosion,
the temperature near the center

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.080
was believed to be between
5,400 and 7,200 Fahrenheit.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:20.770
This is a picture of stone
steps at the entrance of a bank.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.718
The intense heat turned
the steps whiteish,

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:27.630
and the stone under the
person sitting there

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:31.323
waiting for the bank
to open remained dark.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.260
This tricycle belonged
to a three-year-old boy,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.350
Shinichi Tetsutani, who died in the attack

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:48.150
while he was playing on his
favorite toy, his tricycle.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.770
Considering the iron
melts at 2,700 Fahrenheit,

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:56.035
people's skin who were
outside near the hypocenter

00:06:56.035 --> 00:06:59.553
must have been charcoaled instantly.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.280
The atomic bomb differs from other bombs

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.680
because it emits radiation,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.250
which causes cellular and
genetic abnormalities.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.100
The vast majority of
people exposed to radiation

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.753
within 0.6 miles died even
if they were unharmed.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.920
Also, many people are believed
to be damaged by exposure

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.490
to residual radiation from the fallout,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.200
so-called deadly ash,

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.440
and rain mixed with radioactive materials,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:35.943
so-called black rain.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.350
Radiation can cause
various long-term diseases

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.020
such as leukemia, thyroid cancer,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.320
breast cancer, and colorectal cancer.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.993
Even now, it's affecting many people.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:57.150
Although the exact
number is still unknown,

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.910
140,000 people in Hiroshima

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.520
and over 70,000 people in Nagasaki

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.613
are estimated have died
by the end of the year.

00:08:15.149 --> 00:08:20.149
On July, 2017, a global
agreement to ban nuclear weapons

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.850
known as the Treaty on the
Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:28.110
or TPNW was adopted at the United Nations,

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:33.110
which entered into force
on January 22nd, 2021.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.910
Before the treaty's adoption,

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:40.080
nuclear weapons were the only
weapons of mass destruction

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.960
not subject to a comprehensive ban

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.763
despite their humanitarian and
environmental consequences.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.590
It prohibits nations from
developing, testing, producing,

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:57.590
manufacturing, transferring,
processing, stockpiling, using,

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.170
or threatening to use nuclear weapons

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:02.010
or allowing nuclear weapons

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:05.340
to be stationed on their territory.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.090
It also prohibits them from
assisting, encouraging,

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:13.473
or inducing anyone to engage
in any of these activities.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:18.513
The Nobel Peace Prize 2017 was awarded

00:09:18.513 --> 00:09:22.440
to the International Campaign
to Abolish Nuclear Weapons

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.495
or ICAN for its work to draw attention

00:09:26.495 --> 00:09:30.770
to the catastrophic humanitarian
consequences of any use

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:35.490
of nuclear weapons or for
its groundbreaking efforts

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.023
to achieve a treaty-based
prohibition of such weapons.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:50.930
There are now 13,000 nuclear
warheads in the world,

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:56.340
which is enough to wipe out
every living thing on the planet

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.620
multiple times over.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.050
More than 90% of them belong

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.213
to the United States and Russia.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.057
My mother and I are here to speak to you

00:10:07.057 --> 00:10:12.049
not because we want to make
you feel guilty or sorry,

00:10:12.049 --> 00:10:15.610
but because we hope that
we can work together

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.783
to create a more peaceful world.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.650
The average age of hibakusha in Hiroshima

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.175
is now over 84 years old.

00:10:25.175 --> 00:10:27.393
My mother is also 84.

00:10:28.503 --> 00:10:30.265
In the near future,

00:10:30.265 --> 00:10:34.050
we will not be able to
hear directly from them

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.783
so let's invite Toshiko
to share her story.

00:10:38.223 --> 00:10:43.223
(audience applauding)
Thank you.

00:10:44.271 --> 00:10:45.104
(Reiko Tashiro speaks Japanese)

00:10:45.104 --> 00:10:48.243
- Hello, my name is Toshiko Tanaka.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:53.070
I survived the atomic
bombing in Hiroshima.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:58.070
Hibakusha is how we call all
the survivors in Japanese.

00:10:58.533 --> 00:11:03.533
I've been creating enamel mural
artwork for over 50 years.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.320
It wasn't easy for me to talk about,

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.290
but I'm going to tell you today.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.830
I tried not to remember what had happened

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:18.830
in Hiroshima on August the 6th, 1945.

00:11:18.836 --> 00:11:22.263
I thought people would not understand me.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.950
I would not ever talk to anybody

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:29.940
about my personal experience
of the atomic bombing,

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.090
not even to my own family.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:36.600
I started to open up
and share this horrible,

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:40.003
this experience when I turned 70.

00:11:41.074 --> 00:11:42.630
It is a miracle for me

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:45.843
to be able to tell you my story today.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:49.170
Hopefully we are all here

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:53.703
to stop something like this
from ever happening again.

00:11:55.869 --> 00:12:00.540
Originally, my family
lived only 500 meters,

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:05.253
540 yards from ground
zero where the bomb hit.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:10.320
But just one week before
the atomic bombing,

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.563
we moved 2.3 kilometers away,

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:19.020
1.4 miles away from ground zero.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.290
Because of this, I am alive today,

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:27.290
even though I was burned
and exposed to radiation.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.083
When I was just six years old,

00:12:30.083 --> 00:12:33.553
the gigantic mushroom
cloud was right above me.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:39.710
At 8:15 AM, I was on my way to school.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:43.410
Somebody shouted. (speaks Japanese)

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.892
I looked up and saw a tremendous flash.

00:12:46.892 --> 00:12:49.233
It was like a million lights.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:54.270
Everything went white. I couldn't see.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.153
I covered my face with my right arm.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:03.637
Heat burned my head, my
neck, and my right arm.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:09.590
Then suddenly everything
went dark, like nighttime.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:15.063
Hot sand dust blew up. It covered the Sun.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.537
I couldn't see, but the dust cloud

00:13:18.537 --> 00:13:20.733
was like a giant mushroom.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.243
My mouth was full of dust.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.810
What was happening? What should I do?

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:32.133
I can't forget that
terrible crunchy sand taste.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:38.180
Soon my burns began to swell.
The pain was incredible.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.130
I cried all the way home,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:48.033
but our household very
damaged when I got there.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:54.530
Even though my mother was alive,
she could not recognize me.

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:59.970
My hair was burned. I was covered in ash.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.583
My clothes were destroyed.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:06.423
That night, I was close to death.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:11.853
Many doctors were dead.
There were no hospitals left.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:17.043
Survival depended on how
strong you were and how lucky.

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:21.243
My younger sister had bad head wounds.

00:14:22.301 --> 00:14:25.200
My father was away in the army.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.593
Our roof was mostly gone,

00:14:28.593 --> 00:14:33.593
but suddenly I looked up and
saw a small patch of blue sky.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.790
Although I was in pain, I
thought, "This is so beautiful."

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:43.680
That blue sky stayed with me.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.680
It had given me the will to
live as if the heavens said,

00:14:51.427 --> 00:14:55.080
"Don't worry, there will be a tomorrow."

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.010
This is why I continued
to live life positively,

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.743
even through many hardships.

00:15:03.831 --> 00:15:08.100
(claps sharply) It took only
one second for a single bomb

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:13.100
to the destroy the city of
Hiroshima and 140,000 lives

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:21.320
and another bomb to kill
70,000 more in Nagasaki.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.842
All my classmates were killed.

00:15:24.842 --> 00:15:29.842
My younger aunt left home that
morning and never came back.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.303
Her body was never found.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:38.373
Every image of that terrible day remained.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:45.830
My generation will be the last
to tell you about this event

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:48.600
as direct witnesses.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:50.103
This is what I saw.

00:15:51.544 --> 00:15:54.420
After I got home that day,

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.013
I saw a large crowd of dying people.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:03.600
They were walking in
procession near my house.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:04.953
Some were orphans.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:09.270
Men, women, and children alike

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:13.290
were almost naked with burned clothes.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:17.313
They walked in silence
with outstretched arms.

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.770
Burned peeled skin hung from
their tips of their fingers.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.873
They were like ghosts.

00:16:26.543 --> 00:16:31.260
Even today, whenever I
see barbecued tomatoes,

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:36.260
it reminds me of that
terrible scene of death.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:42.753
Like tomatoes, human skin
peels off easily when burned.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:48.150
I have another strong memory of that time.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.752
I was unconscious for a few days.

00:16:50.752 --> 00:16:54.753
When I woke up, there was
a strong smell in the air.

00:16:55.789 --> 00:16:59.730
It was like burning rotten fish.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.734
Even now I can still smell it,

00:17:02.734 --> 00:17:05.730
but the smell wasn't the fish.

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:10.730
It was the smell of the
human bodies being cremated

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.453
in city parks and the school grounds.

00:17:16.553 --> 00:17:19.704
Even though our family had nothing,

00:17:19.704 --> 00:17:23.823
my mother sheltered many
people in our house.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.440
One was her cousin, Kenzo Matsuki.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.470
He came to our house with a burned bucket

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:36.470
containing the skull of a
woman he wishes to bury.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.900
The skull belonged to his aunt.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.833
His aunt was burned
alive in her own house.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:48.540
People around her, not to do anything

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:53.540
because she was trapped
badly in the burning house.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:59.223
They themselves had to escape from fire.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:05.450
They begged for her forgiveness
while departing her.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:13.040
I also remember a 15 old
girl who came to our house.

00:18:13.667 --> 00:18:18.667
On her back, she carried her
badly burned younger sister.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:23.870
The burned girl survived,
but the older girl did not.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:27.873
She had been exposed to massive radiation.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:33.420
My husband's uncle was an English teacher

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:36.090
and a devout Christian.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:41.090
He used to paint beautiful
pictures and believed in peace,

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.723
but the bomb killed him

00:18:43.723 --> 00:18:48.723
and all his six family
members, including his baby.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.980
Grieving their loss, my husband adopted

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.953
his uncle's family name Tanaka.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:01.940
My husband has now died, but
I still carry this last name.

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:06.510
In those days, the city of Hiroshima

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:10.590
and the surface of the rivers
which run through the city

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:14.043
were entirely covered by people's bodies.

00:19:17.391 --> 00:19:20.820
There were also 12
young American prisoners

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.133
of war in Hiroshima.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:30.290
They also died from the bomb
dropped by their own country.

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:37.490
Later, something weird started
to happen in Hiroshima.

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.243
People with no injuries started to die.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.340
No one knew then,

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:48.213
but they had been exposed
to massive radiation.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:51.810
Exactly how much radiation

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:55.653
the atomic bombs produced
is still unknown.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:20:02.060
Do you know the famous story
of Sadako and her paper cranes?

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:04.740
Sadako was four years younger than me,

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:06.933
and we went to the same school.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:12.600
She survived the atomic bomb
when she was two years old,

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:15.963
but died of leukemia at age 12.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.133
There were many other
children like Sadako.

00:20:21.542 --> 00:20:25.863
In my case, radiation symptoms
started when I was 12.

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.383
My white blood cell count was abnormal.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:37.263
I suffered constant fever and
fatigue and sometimes fainted.

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:42.630
I always had mouth ulcers
and bruises around my mouth.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.703
It was hard to eat.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.476
Even today, when I am very tired,

00:20:48.476 --> 00:20:53.476
I have painful sores and can't sleep.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:56.973
I sometimes bleed from my colon.

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:01.410
I have had three ribs broken

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:04.890
and knee surgery and cataract surgery.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:07.410
The Japanese government decided

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:12.410
to certify atomic bomb victims
only within two kilometers,

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:18.663
1.24 mile radius of ground zero.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:24.620
Since I lived 300 meters,
328 yards beyond this radius

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:33.600
my symptoms are not officially certified

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:35.973
as atomic bomb related.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:41.250
Some other victims have
tried to sue the government,

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:46.250
but instead I decided to
spend the rest of my life

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.053
on my art and testimony.

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:55.053
For more than six years
after the end of the war,

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:01.260
the American government
hid the radiation damage

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.170
by blocking the media.

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:09.170
In those days, the country
was competing against the USSR

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:13.560
in nuclear arms development

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:17.373
and wanted to carry out
numerous nuclear tests.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:22.800
It was also developing
nuclear power generation

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.220
under the name of peace facilitation

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:30.270
so the damage caused by radioactivity

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:34.290
was uncomfortable truth
for the government.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:39.030
So without knowing, citizens
drank contaminated water

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:44.030
and ate vegetables grown
in contaminated grounds.

00:22:44.413 --> 00:22:49.413
Later, many relatives and
friends died from leukemia

00:22:51.783 --> 00:22:55.083
and other forms of cancer.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:00.000
Those days, hibakusha also
suffered discrimination

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.833
in Japanese society.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:08.283
In those those days, hibakusha
women had trouble marrying

00:23:08.283 --> 00:23:12.393
because their babies
often had birth defect.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.690
Fortunately, I was able to marry

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:18.270
and had a happy family life.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:23.270
My husband did not seem to
mind that I was a hibakusha,

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:30.110
but when our first child,
Reiko, was born, he was scared.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:35.433
He seriously checked the
baby's fingers and toes.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:39.810
Then I realized that he had been worried

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:44.643
about the damage of the bomb on our baby.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:46.980
Not just my husband,

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.233
but all parents those days were worried.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:55.291
Up until now, there still
had not been enough research

00:23:55.291 --> 00:24:00.291
on the impact of radioactivity
on the second generation.

00:24:01.483 --> 00:24:06.483
No doctors can give any
legitimate diagnosis.

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:10.020
There is no proof that
the second generation

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.780
can maintain their health.

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:16.978
The hibakusha continue to
feel sorry for their children

00:24:16.978 --> 00:24:21.436
and have to carry this
psychological burden

00:24:21.436 --> 00:24:23.269
for their entire life.

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:27.630
Before the bomb, our family line

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:32.630
generally had been
blessed with good health.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.690
However, after the bomb,
three of my sister's daughters

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:40.180
who are second-generation hibakusha

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:44.010
were afflicted with thyroid disease.

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:48.210
One of them had surgery
to remove thyroid cancer

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:50.103
at the age of 22.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.770
My younger brother,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.920
who is also a second-generation hibakusha

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:58.770
had surgery to remove colon cancer

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:01.323
when he was still quite young.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:06.760
Doctors say there is no evidence
to connect these diseases

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:12.790
to the atomic bomb, but we
hibakusha have always felt

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:19.083
the high possibility of
side effects of the bombing.

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:24.603
Nobody on this planet should
suffer this same tragedy.

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:31.490
Since 2007, I have made
four round-the-world voyages

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:34.353
on Peace Boat, which is sponsored.

00:25:37.995 --> 00:25:41.213
It is a Japan-based international NGO.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.874
I learned more about the
situation of the world

00:25:45.874 --> 00:25:48.210
during these voyages.

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:51.090
I have visited more than 80 countries,

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:54.676
including ones that are
suffering tragic wars

00:25:54.676 --> 00:25:59.676
and the ones that suffer
damage from climate change.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:02.730
I learned that nuclear weapons

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:05.443
will lead the Earth to destruction.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:10.503
They are inhumane and should
never be allowed on Earth.

00:26:11.981 --> 00:26:16.981
In 2009, Dr. Kathleen Sullivan
and Mr. Robert Croonquist

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:23.270
who are the founders of NY-based NGO

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:29.000
Youth Arts New York's
Hibakusha Stories initiative

00:26:29.431 --> 00:26:34.431
kindly invited me and
gave me the opportunity

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:39.240
to share my testimony with
students in high schools

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:43.653
and colleges in New York and Oklahoma.

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:49.050
And since I have made 12 visits

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:52.800
in seven years with their program.

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:55.062
The program has reached

00:26:55.062 --> 00:26:58.203
more than 50,000 students and citizens.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:07.040
During that testimony, I
sometimes use my enamel artworks.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:13.040
I believe art is a powerful
tool for peace education.

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:20.809
One day, a junior high school
student in New York City

00:27:20.809 --> 00:27:25.300
who joined the Hibakusha
Stories program asked me

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:29.040
why I made a friend with Americans

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:33.553
even though I was nearly
killed by the atomic bomb.

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:36.737
My answer was this.

00:27:36.737 --> 00:27:39.840
During the war, Japanese
people were taught

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:44.840
to believe that all
Westerners were demons.

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:50.874
In the beginning, it was
very hard to forgive them

00:27:50.874 --> 00:27:54.780
for all the suffering caused by the bomb,

00:27:54.780 --> 00:27:59.780
but I now believe that hatred
only creates a chain of hatred

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:06.090
and it will eventually lead to killings

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:08.910
and destruction by war.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:12.660
Humankind needs to make every effort

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:16.051
to cut off this chain of hatred

00:28:16.051 --> 00:28:20.613
and instead have love
throughout humanity as a whole.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:26.930
In 2010, I started to
take part in a campaign

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:34.004
in which we visited local
officials throughout Japan

00:28:34.004 --> 00:28:37.350
and collected their signatures

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:41.700
agreeing to support the
anti-nuclear policy.

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:45.060
I also created an art piece

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:47.913
for an anti-nuclear artwork project.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:53.960
On March 11th, 2011, there
were a series of incidents

00:28:56.910 --> 00:29:01.910
at the nuclear power
plant in Fukushima, Japan.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.600
I felt a strong urge

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:06.303
to learn why these things are happening.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:12.230
In 2012, I joined a project
to visit the areas damaged

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:21.290
by the Chernobyl nuclear power
plant disaster in Ukraine

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:26.403
in 1986 and its neighboring
country, Belarus.

00:29:28.148 --> 00:29:31.920
At the Chernobyl plant,
the commission work

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:36.300
had been going on for nearly
30 years, but they said

00:29:37.951 --> 00:29:42.951
they might need 40 or 100
more years to finish it.

00:29:45.061 --> 00:29:50.061
Our government has not learned
from either the tragedy

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:55.100
on Daigo Fukuryu Maru or
the Chernobyl disaster.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:59.430
Daigo Fukuryu Maru was
a tuna fishing boat who,

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:04.430
the 23 crew members were
exposed to nuclear fallout

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:09.300
for a nuclear test carried out by the US

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:13.383
in 1954 at Bikini Atoll.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.850
Instead, the Japanese government continues

00:30:17.850 --> 00:30:21.780
to produce radioactive substances

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:25.593
which will create even
more victims of radiation.

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:31.550
Doing so, they are also
depriving their own citizens

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:35.490
of food and a place to live.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:44.253
We do not know when the
disaster in Fukushima will end.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:48.270
There are many people working tirelessly

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:49.980
for a nuclear-free world.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:53.850
For instance, ICAN is a global campaign

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:57.513
to promote the abolishment
of nuclear weapons.

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:03.350
On July 7th, 2017, the world
took the essential step

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.770
it needed to add peace.

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:12.030
However, none of nuclear
states and their allies,

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:17.030
including Japan, has supported the treaty.

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:24.350
I urge the leaders of
the nuclear weapon states

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:30.020
and other nations not to
dismiss the TPNW idealistic

00:31:34.673 --> 00:31:39.673
but to listen to the voice
of people and read the treaty

00:31:40.897 --> 00:31:43.770
and think about it again,

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:48.770
and please think about creating
peace, not going to war.

00:31:51.787 --> 00:31:54.693
Nuclear weapons do not deter war.

00:31:55.590 --> 00:31:59.163
Russia itself is now proving this point.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:06.260
Last year in 2022, Russian President Putin

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:10.713
started the war in Ukraine,

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:16.143
and suggesting the use of nuclear weapons.

00:32:17.153 --> 00:32:20.940
For a survivor who knows
the inhumane tragedy

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:25.940
caused by those weapons,
this is totally unacceptable,

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:32.160
and I feel strong anger and frustrated

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:35.463
that personally could not do anything.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:41.480
Then I received a video
message from Ms. Tamara

00:32:41.617 --> 00:32:46.617
whom I had met 10 years
earlier in my visit to Ukraine.

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:54.050
She strongly asked me, "Ms.
Tanaka, as a bomb survivor,

00:32:55.230 --> 00:33:00.230
you have to keep telling the
world about the terrible things

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:05.127
that would happen if
nuclear weapons were used."

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:11.660
Empowered by her message and
with the support of Peace Boat

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:17.400
and Hiroshima-based NGO and Hiroshima,

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:21.933
I gave a talk on Russian
YouTube channel last year.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:28.923
I hope that hibakushas' voice
has reached Russian people.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:36.060
In November last year, my
daughter and I were invited

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:39.483
to speak at an international conference

00:33:39.483 --> 00:33:42.813
held at Otago University in New Zealand.

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:48.920
I realized how seriously
the nuclear issues

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:54.650
are impacting the Southern Hemisphere

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:00.330
and global South through problems

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:05.330
including nuclear testing
and nuclear waste.

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:12.570
Also, they were developed
and used by the countries

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:19.940
of global North and that
the issues no longer

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:28.623
cannot be solved only by the global North.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:33.630
We must work together
as a global community

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:35.853
to solve these problems.

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:41.840
During World War II, then
US President Harry Truman

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:47.270
decided to drop atomic bombs
without a second thought.

00:34:47.700 --> 00:34:52.679
He said it was necessary in
order to end the war quickly,

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:56.970
but it is believed that the real reason

00:34:56.970 --> 00:34:58.923
was to send a message to the USSR.

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:05.102
One of the soldiers who dropped the bombs

00:35:05.102 --> 00:35:09.183
from the Enola Gay bomber was Jacob Beser.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:11.033
You know what?

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:17.330
Clifton Truman, who is a
grandson of Harry Truman,

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:22.580
and Ari Beser, who is a
grandson of Jacob Beser

00:35:23.433 --> 00:35:26.853
are both good friends of mine today.

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.510
Ari visited me often.

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:35.510
We actually met recently and
spent a good time together.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:40.770
Both Clifton and Ari are working very hard

00:35:40.770 --> 00:35:43.143
toward creating world peace.

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:48.030
If we were still at war,

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:51.540
these good people and I would be enemies.

00:35:51.540 --> 00:35:55.890
If we have close friends
from around the world,

00:35:55.890 --> 00:35:58.620
we will think twice before starting wars

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:03.270
that just seek personal
prosperity and supremacy.

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:05.890
Empathy is an essential trait

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:10.323
for policymakers and citizens alike.

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:16.673
In the past, Japan caused
the attack on Pearl Harbor

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:24.350
and other vigorous wars on aggression

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:29.310
that left tremendous damage,

00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:32.343
especially on countries in Asia.

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:38.570
The Japanese people must reflect on this.

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:43.428
When the atomic bombs were dropped,

00:36:43.428 --> 00:36:48.428
people in Northeast Asia
thought, "Now we are freed."

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:56.150
However, the use of nuclear
weapons shocked the world

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:04.070
by creating a different devastation

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:08.703
that changed the concept of war.

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:13.200
When I apologized to my Malaysian friend

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:18.200
for the Japanese military's
atrocious behaviors in the past,

00:37:22.182 --> 00:37:27.182
he said, "I don't expect
you to apologize to me now,

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:33.770
but I want you to remember
what happened in the past.

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:41.330
Let's try our best to prevent
another war from happening."

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:47.220
He's a person whose grandfather

00:37:47.220 --> 00:37:49.923
was killed by Japanese soldiers.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:56.090
We as citizens must not
create leaders of a country

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:01.417
that start war for the sake

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:07.670
of territorial expansion
and better economy.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:13.710
It will lead to an arms race

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:17.590
and eventually to the
use of nuclear weapons,

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:22.220
which will cause horrible devastation

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:25.683
and the end of the world.

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:31.520
We are the crew members of
a ship called Planet Earth.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.320
If the crew members fight each other

00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:40.650
for food and a good place

00:38:40.650 --> 00:38:45.650
the ship eventually drift
away and be shipwrecked

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:52.330
and none of us will survive.

00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:58.080
Now is the time for the
world to help each other

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:02.103
and create peace through diplomacy.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:10.110
To conclude my story, I
have one request for you,

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:12.840
the young generation.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:17.220
Please choose the path
to coexist regardless

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:21.003
of your differences including
nationality and race.

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:26.160
If in the future there are
disputes among the nations,

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:31.160
please try to solve them
with words instead of wars.

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:37.850
Once we start wars, we can
only each other as enemies,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:44.690
not as humans, and we
kill enemies with ease.

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:50.310
Nuclear weapons will lead
the Earth to distraction.

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:54.333
They are inhumane and should
never be allowed on Earth.

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:02.532
I know one day we will live
in a nuclear-free world

00:40:02.532 --> 00:40:06.480
and a beautiful blue sky
will continue to shine

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:09.900
above the heads of our future generation.

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:12.060
Thank you for your kind attention.

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:16.323
I will be happy to answer
your questions. Thank you.

00:40:16.323 --> 00:40:17.257
(audience applauding)

00:40:17.257 --> 00:40:18.900
- [Attendee] Thank you so much.

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:21.990
Thank you so much to both of
you for coming to speak with us

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:24.240
and thank you so much
for sharing your story.

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:30.240
Yeah, I'm very moved by it,
and it, yeah, it means a lot

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:32.670
that you came to speak
with us, so thank you.

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:37.670
My question is a bit
departing from your story

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:41.807
about the nuclear bomb
itself or the atomic bomb

00:40:41.807 --> 00:40:46.290
and more about, I mean, you're
one of the last generation

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:51.290
who will remember and who
was there for the bombing,

00:40:51.419 --> 00:40:55.260
but you're also one of the
last generations who remembers

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:58.641
how the city was like before the bombing.

00:40:58.641 --> 00:41:01.230
And I'm wondering if there's
anything that you'd like us

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:06.230
to remember or carry about
the city before the bomb,

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:10.950
the city, the people, that
you'd like us to carry with us.

00:41:10.950 --> 00:41:12.797
Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:41:12.797 --> 00:41:16.964
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:41:30.932 --> 00:41:35.182
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:41:54.999 --> 00:41:59.999
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:15.450
- So, someone once said that
when they went to Hiroshima

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:19.290
that they saw the Peace Park
in the middle of the city

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:22.260
and they said, "Oh, it's a good thing

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.183
that the bomb fell on a park"

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:29.010
without understanding the
reality, which was that in fact

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:30.720
that was the center of the city

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.093
where ordinary people made their lives.

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:39.270
And that was of course
completely destroyed and changed

00:42:39.270 --> 00:42:41.493
as a result of the dropping of the bomb.

00:42:42.990 --> 00:42:47.990
So that person's kind of
misunderstanding was suggestive

00:42:49.623 --> 00:42:54.063
of the enormity of what happened.

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:59.750
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:13.800
- So the place where the
Peace Memorial now stands

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:17.763
in Hiroshima is precisely
where her kindergarten was.

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.500
And so she was supposed to
graduate from the kindergarten

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:23.333
and go to the school.

00:43:23.333 --> 00:43:25.590
All of her classmates
who attended that school

00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:27.003
died in the bomb.

00:43:28.606 --> 00:43:32.856
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:43:41.315 --> 00:43:46.315
- So it was a beautiful town
and of course in many ways

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.680
there were lots of fun
things about the town,

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:51.810
but as a result of all
of her classmates dying,

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:55.113
it also became a place that
she never could go again.

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:58.407
- Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:04.110
- [Attendee] Thank you so much
for this inspiring speech.

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:06.020
It's very powerful and I...

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:10.590
Like, sometimes it just
almost bring tears to my eyes,

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:13.260
and I really appreciate this opportunity.

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:18.260
So I'm sure an audience really
get your inner strengths.

00:44:19.530 --> 00:44:21.420
And that's what I'm thinking,

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:24.120
how you can share with the
younger generation, you know,

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:29.120
under this kind of a circumstance
what make you to stand up

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:34.260
in the middle of this darkness, pain?

00:44:34.260 --> 00:44:38.970
What keep you still be
so hopeful and strong?

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:43.620
How you build your resilience
and inner strengths and peace,

00:44:43.620 --> 00:44:46.050
that's what I want to know

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:48.720
if you can share with our audience,

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:51.780
especially younger
generation, thank you so much.

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:58.267
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:45:07.716 --> 00:45:11.966
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:45:32.023 --> 00:45:37.023
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:45:56.965 --> 00:46:01.965
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:09.080
- So after the bomb was dropped
and the city was destroyed,

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:13.230
she as a child remembers
looking up at the sky

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:16.200
and seeing that it was
this beautiful blue sky.

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:19.530
And even though her body was in pain,

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:22.890
she and all of the adults
were of course unable

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:24.420
to look at something like the sky,

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:27.097
but as a child she could look
up and see the sky and think,

00:46:27.097 --> 00:46:29.970
"Oh, it's the same sky as yesterday.

00:46:29.970 --> 00:46:31.560
If yesterday's sky is here,

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:33.210
then tomorrow's sky will be here.

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:34.921
There will be a tomorrow."

00:46:34.921 --> 00:46:37.770
And having lived for 84 years, you know,

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:41.280
she's experienced all sorts of
trials and difficult things,

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:44.550
but she's always felt that she knows

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:48.513
that tomorrow will come,
there'll be a better tomorrow.

00:46:49.650 --> 00:46:53.513
So I think that's the...
- Thank you, thank you.

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:56.160
- [Attendee] Like everyone else,

00:46:56.160 --> 00:46:59.250
I want to say thank you so
much for sharing your story.

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:00.810
It was really moving.

00:47:00.810 --> 00:47:03.660
And my question is that you mentioned

00:47:03.660 --> 00:47:06.030
how you didn't tell your
story until you were 70.

00:47:06.030 --> 00:47:10.140
And so I wanted to ask, how
did you make that decision?

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:11.550
Why did it take so long?

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:14.460
And then how did you then decide

00:47:14.460 --> 00:47:16.373
that you wanted to tell the world?

00:47:18.066 --> 00:47:22.483
(Reiko Tashiro speaking in Japanese)

00:47:26.822 --> 00:47:31.822
- (speaks Japanese) Good
question. (speaks Japanese)

00:47:51.017 --> 00:47:56.017
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:48:14.948 --> 00:48:19.948
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:48:39.646 --> 00:48:44.646
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:55.986
- So when she was younger,
her feeling was that

00:48:55.986 --> 00:48:57.990
even if she talked about what happened,

00:48:57.990 --> 00:48:59.430
she wouldn't really be able to have,

00:48:59.430 --> 00:49:01.050
no one would be able to understand

00:49:01.050 --> 00:49:02.943
who hadn't actually lived through it.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:08.920
And she also felt that she
didn't want to remember

00:49:10.470 --> 00:49:12.630
because all of her classmates had died.

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:14.730
She didn't want to have to think about it.

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:20.600
And she felt also that
some kind of discomfort

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:23.673
towards the people who had died.

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:28.890
But so when she was 70,
the thing that changed

00:49:28.890 --> 00:49:31.800
was she went on the Peace
Boat to South America,

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:33.600
to Venezuela.
- Yes, yes.

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:38.600
- And in Venezuela, a mayor in
one of the towns she visited

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:41.700
said, "As somebody who lived through this,

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:45.867
you have an obligation, a
responsibility to talk about it."

00:49:46.830 --> 00:49:51.180
And she decided that if
she had a responsibility

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:53.880
to speak about it, then
perhaps the people who died

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:57.513
would be able to forgive
her for speaking about it,

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:00.780
this horrible thing that
had happened to them.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:04.660
So that became the
catalyst for her decision

00:50:05.734 --> 00:50:06.990
to begin speaking openly about it.

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:08.190
And the first time she spoke

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:10.920
was indeed on Venezuelan television.

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:12.183
- Television, teleSUR.

00:50:13.646 --> 00:50:14.910
- Tele...
- teleSUR. (speaks Japanese)

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:19.170
- teleSUR apparently is the
TV station in Venezuela.

00:50:19.170 --> 00:50:23.871
So that was the first
time she spoke about it.

00:50:23.871 --> 00:50:25.650
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:50:25.650 --> 00:50:29.113
- Peace Boat. (speaks in Japanese)

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:35.400
- So since then she's been on
four trips on the Peace Boat

00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:37.350
and visited many different countries

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:39.540
and been able to have the opportunity

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:42.993
to speak about her experiences
all over the world.

00:50:43.991 --> 00:50:46.609
(Toshiko Tanaka speaks Japanese)

00:50:46.609 --> 00:50:47.757
- [Attendee] I'd like to say thank you

00:50:47.757 --> 00:50:50.130
for the work that you do.

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:55.130
It's important, and also it
was delightful to see your art,

00:50:56.910 --> 00:51:01.910
which challenges the mind, I
think, to understand it fully.

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:07.293
I didn't know that John
Hersey taught at Yale,

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:12.000
and for those of you who
are two generations younger

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:16.920
than I am, I commend his
book "Hiroshima" to you.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:21.870
He did the research. He
was a journalist also.

00:51:21.870 --> 00:51:24.900
And he did the research
and the book was published,

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:29.900
I think, in '46 or '47,
and it doesn't soft pedal.

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:38.103
It is grizzly at times to
understand what happened.

00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:41.607
He also wrote a book
called "A Bell for Adano,"

00:51:42.660 --> 00:51:46.710
which won a Pulitzer Prize
and is a happier war story.

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:49.287
So maybe read that after
you read "Hiroshima."

00:51:52.202 --> 00:51:56.369
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:52:08.884 --> 00:52:09.717
(Daniel Botsman speaking Japanese)

00:52:09.717 --> 00:52:14.280
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:17.354
- John Hersey (speaks Japanese)

00:52:17.354 --> 00:52:18.187
(Daniel Botsman speaking Japanese)

00:52:18.187 --> 00:52:22.437
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:52:31.344 --> 00:52:32.836
(Daniel Botsman speaking Japanese)

00:52:32.836 --> 00:52:36.450
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:41.450
- So John Hersey's grandson
actually has visited her

00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:45.060
and spent time, they have a connection.

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:49.530
And he actually recently, this
is me adding this, I'm sorry,

00:52:49.530 --> 00:52:52.740
but he made a documentary
film a few years ago also

00:52:52.740 --> 00:52:57.740
about peace education and the experience

00:52:57.840 --> 00:52:59.890
of people like Tanaka-san

00:53:01.530 --> 00:53:04.474
so the Hersey connection
is an important one.

00:53:04.474 --> 00:53:08.724
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:12.137
- So she's very happy to hear

00:53:12.137 --> 00:53:16.250
that he's remembered here, too, his work.

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:20.261
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:53:20.261 --> 00:53:22.110
- [Attendee] Thank you very much.

00:53:22.110 --> 00:53:25.380
I found it very, very touching.

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:30.380
I'm curious about how the
disasters that struck Japan,

00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:36.500
Hiroshima, Nagasaki, how they're
treated in the classroom.

00:53:39.386 --> 00:53:44.040
And what's age appropriate in Japan

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:46.083
for the teaching of this history?

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:51.157
As the American historian
David McCullough said,

00:53:51.157 --> 00:53:54.330
"Americans aren't particularly
good at their history."

00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:58.710
And I'm not sure how many Americans

00:53:58.710 --> 00:54:01.927
are aware of what happened
to Japan, thank you.

00:54:04.262 --> 00:54:08.429
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:54:20.333 --> 00:54:24.583
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:54:44.964 --> 00:54:49.964
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:55:09.995 --> 00:55:12.240
- So for six years after the war,

00:55:12.240 --> 00:55:17.240
it was prohibited to
discuss what had happened.

00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:20.580
And then in the 1950s there
was gradually the beginnings

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:25.580
of some educational efforts
around the bombings.

00:55:27.618 --> 00:55:31.380
But recently there's been yet more change,

00:55:31.380 --> 00:55:35.700
and partly as a result
of the war in Ukraine

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:39.720
and Russia's invasion there's
now an effort domestically

00:55:39.720 --> 00:55:44.100
in Japan to kind of help to
encourage ordinary people

00:55:44.100 --> 00:55:47.610
to open up to the possibility of Japan

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:51.393
becoming a nuclear power and arming.

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:57.350
And so the struggles over these
issues continue within Japan

00:55:58.080 --> 00:55:59.820
and have shifted backwards and forwards.

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:04.820
But in the current moment,
various things have happened

00:56:04.920 --> 00:56:08.310
that are quite concerning,
including, for example,

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:13.310
one of the most important
post-war comic books

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:15.750
is called "Barefoot Gen,"
and it tells the story

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:17.070
of what happened in Hiroshima

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:19.863
from the perspective of
a boy and his family.

00:56:21.450 --> 00:56:25.650
And recently the teaching of that book

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:29.340
has become very controversial.

00:56:29.340 --> 00:56:32.220
So it was used in schools in the past,

00:56:32.220 --> 00:56:35.220
but recently in Hiroshima they've said

00:56:35.220 --> 00:56:37.670
that they're not gonna
use it in schools anymore.

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:41.370
So these things are
domestically in Japan, too,

00:56:41.370 --> 00:56:44.580
always being contested and disputed

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:46.833
and things go backwards and forwards.

00:56:49.273 --> 00:56:53.490
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:54.930
- Whenever there's a change of government,

00:56:54.930 --> 00:56:56.193
it seems to shift.

00:56:58.230 --> 00:56:59.063
- [Attendee] First of all,

00:56:59.063 --> 00:57:01.230
thank you so much for sharing your story.

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:04.503
I feel really lucky to be able to hear it.

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:08.280
I was lucky enough to
visit Hiroshima last month

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:13.280
on a study abroad and saw the
museum there and was saddened

00:57:16.110 --> 00:57:20.460
and inspired by it in a way
that I feel I never could be

00:57:20.460 --> 00:57:23.550
at any such museum here in the US.

00:57:23.550 --> 00:57:27.030
So I'm wondering from
you, what's the best way

00:57:27.030 --> 00:57:30.540
that we can share your
story here in the US

00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:32.160
with the generations to come?

00:57:33.133 --> 00:57:37.300
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

00:57:49.450 --> 00:57:53.700
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

00:58:14.899 --> 00:58:19.899
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:58:39.953 --> 00:58:44.953
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

00:58:46.731 --> 00:58:51.731
- So as a result of Putin's
invasion of Ukraine,

00:58:54.120 --> 00:58:56.340
the current moment is
one in which these issues

00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:59.370
have become so much more
difficult to discuss.

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:02.730
And there are certainly
people in Japan and elsewhere

00:59:02.730 --> 00:59:07.560
who say that the kind of people

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:09.897
who talk about abolishing nuclear weapons

00:59:09.897 --> 00:59:12.450
are the kind of people who won't fight

00:59:12.450 --> 00:59:14.883
and will just run away, they're cowards.

00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:19.290
But from the perspective of hibakusha,

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:22.510
somebody who lived through
the experience of being bombed

00:59:23.640 --> 00:59:28.640
with nuclear weapons, her
position is that the true hero

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:34.500
is somebody who doesn't
fight, refuses to kill,

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:39.500
and insists on talking and
talking through the problem

00:59:40.710 --> 00:59:45.060
until there's a resolution, so.

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:46.367
- Thank you.

00:59:46.367 --> 00:59:47.850
- [Attendee] Hello, thank you very much

00:59:47.850 --> 00:59:49.653
for sharing this story.

00:59:50.580 --> 00:59:54.030
During the talk, Tanaka-san,
you mentioned that one

00:59:54.030 --> 00:59:57.870
of the ways to potentially
avoid future nuclear conflicts

00:59:57.870 --> 01:00:00.480
is for empathy between people.

01:00:00.480 --> 01:00:02.790
And I just wanted to hear your thoughts on

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:05.850
if you had any ideas on
how you might go about

01:00:05.850 --> 01:00:07.477
seeding more empathy in the world,

01:00:07.477 --> 01:00:11.550
and particularly following
your efforts on the Peace Boat

01:00:11.550 --> 01:00:14.370
and other peace-building activities.

01:00:14.370 --> 01:00:15.983
Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:00:17.834 --> 01:00:22.001
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

01:00:37.539 --> 01:00:41.789
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

01:01:03.108 --> 01:01:08.108
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

01:01:27.746 --> 01:01:32.746
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

01:01:33.812 --> 01:01:38.520
- So as she mentioned a
little bit in her main speech,

01:01:38.520 --> 01:01:40.770
she thinks about the world

01:01:40.770 --> 01:01:45.123
as a kind of tiny place
in a huge universe,

01:01:45.986 --> 01:01:49.440
but one in which we are
all on board together

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:51.573
as crew members on this kind of ship.

01:01:52.440 --> 01:01:56.310
And if the crew members
on a ship start fighting

01:01:56.310 --> 01:01:58.710
with each other about who gets the food

01:01:58.710 --> 01:02:02.684
or who gets the best
broom, then it's inevitable

01:02:02.684 --> 01:02:05.851
that the ship will go off course.

01:02:05.851 --> 01:02:08.913
And when that happens, everyone dies.

01:02:10.020 --> 01:02:13.530
So from her point of view,
the current moment we're in

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:17.250
is a turning point where
those possibilities

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:19.323
are becoming more and more pronounced.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:25.790
And so she encourages us all
to think about that reality.

01:02:32.796 --> 01:02:34.080
- And the imagination.

01:02:34.080 --> 01:02:39.000
- Oh, and use your imaginations
to think about things

01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:43.030
in that way so as to prevent
the terrible outcomes

01:02:45.330 --> 01:02:48.926
that are likely to occur otherwise.

01:02:48.926 --> 01:02:53.176
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:14.760
- So she has very high hopes

01:03:14.760 --> 01:03:17.253
for all of the brilliant
Yale students out there.

01:03:18.450 --> 01:03:22.560
She obviously hasn't met you.
I'm just kidding. (laughs)

01:03:22.560 --> 01:03:24.780
No, for all of you,
she has very high hopes

01:03:24.780 --> 01:03:29.780
that you will work towards
achieving a better,

01:03:30.390 --> 01:03:31.710
more sustainable world,

01:03:31.710 --> 01:03:34.620
one in which people do
indeed speak to each other

01:03:34.620 --> 01:03:37.170
rather than fighting with each other

01:03:37.170 --> 01:03:38.403
when there are problems.

01:03:39.505 --> 01:03:42.150
- Thank you very much.
- Thank you. (speaks Japanese)

01:03:42.150 --> 01:03:47.150
So if there's one more question
anybody has, and then...

01:03:48.030 --> 01:03:49.410
- [Attendee] Thank you so much of course

01:03:49.410 --> 01:03:51.540
for speaking to us today.

01:03:51.540 --> 01:03:53.790
Of course, like, it's important,

01:03:53.790 --> 01:03:56.430
you realized that it's important
that you share this story

01:03:56.430 --> 01:03:59.340
so that it's not repeated
in the world again.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.080
But what I wanted to ask was that,

01:04:01.080 --> 01:04:05.940
how after a life of choosing
to not speak of this

01:04:05.940 --> 01:04:08.580
do you balance and take
care of yourself reliving it

01:04:08.580 --> 01:04:10.110
so that other people don't have to,

01:04:10.110 --> 01:04:13.950
while also not sort of
traumatizing yourself over

01:04:13.950 --> 01:04:15.510
and over again and reliving it?

01:04:15.510 --> 01:04:18.488
So how do you make that
balance in your head

01:04:18.488 --> 01:04:22.470
and take care of yourself while
also speaking on the story

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:23.520
to make sure that other people

01:04:23.520 --> 01:04:26.370
don't have to go through
the same thing again?

01:04:26.370 --> 01:04:27.203
Thank you.

01:04:30.449 --> 01:04:34.616
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

01:04:42.337 --> 01:04:47.337
- (speaks Japanese) Good
question. (speaks Japanese)

01:05:07.031 --> 01:05:12.031
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

01:05:31.940 --> 01:05:34.660
- So she said it's an
extremely good question

01:05:35.820 --> 01:05:40.820
and that it is really hard to
talk about these experiences,

01:05:43.800 --> 01:05:46.710
but she says recently her
feelings have started to change

01:05:46.710 --> 01:05:50.280
and that she's buoyed by the possibility

01:05:50.280 --> 01:05:55.140
that by speaking about things,
that it may actually lead

01:05:55.140 --> 01:05:58.230
to some positive change
and move the world,

01:05:58.230 --> 01:06:00.687
even if it's ever so
slightly in the direction

01:06:00.687 --> 01:06:04.830
of abandoning nuclear weapons.

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:09.480
And as I'm sure many of
you know, the G7 meeting

01:06:09.480 --> 01:06:12.060
will be taking place in
Hiroshima fairly soon,

01:06:12.060 --> 01:06:16.524
and she's really hopeful
that maybe, just maybe,

01:06:16.524 --> 01:06:19.530
the leaders at that
conference will be inspired

01:06:19.530 --> 01:06:23.100
by their visit to Hiroshima
and take some action

01:06:23.100 --> 01:06:25.263
to address this profound problem.

01:06:26.149 --> 01:06:30.399
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

01:06:53.911 --> 01:06:58.744
- She wants you to know that
there are many people who die

01:06:59.910 --> 01:07:02.700
having experienced what
happened in Hiroshima

01:07:02.700 --> 01:07:05.609
without ever being able to speak about it.

01:07:05.609 --> 01:07:10.609
So she has a friend whose
marriage basically fell apart.

01:07:12.690 --> 01:07:15.960
I mean, she wasn't able to get married

01:07:15.960 --> 01:07:20.800
because of the fact that
she'd been irradiated

01:07:21.990 --> 01:07:26.310
and she remained single
for her entire life.

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:30.120
She's now 85 and she was moved

01:07:30.120 --> 01:07:34.113
into a psychiatric home and died there.

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:39.780
And she was never in her
entire life able to speak

01:07:39.780 --> 01:07:44.780
about what had happened
to her during the bombing.

01:07:46.853 --> 01:07:51.390
So because of that, Tanaka-san
feels as though she needs

01:07:51.390 --> 01:07:56.390
to speak so that people
can hear what happened.

01:08:00.420 --> 01:08:02.040
- Thank you.

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:04.925
- Please join with me in
thanking Tanaka-san again

01:08:04.925 --> 01:08:07.925
for this incredible speech and talk.

01:08:08.770 --> 01:08:09.768
(audience applauding)

01:08:09.768 --> 01:08:13.210
Oh, I'm sorry, one last question.

01:08:13.210 --> 01:08:17.640
- [Attendee] Hi. Thank you
for the very inspiring talk.

01:08:17.640 --> 01:08:21.243
So I'm gonna ask a question
on behalf of my daughter.

01:08:23.430 --> 01:08:25.660
She would like to know
what you were eating

01:08:26.533 --> 01:08:28.770
during the time World War II

01:08:28.770 --> 01:08:33.615
because she has a grandma in Tokyo.

01:08:33.615 --> 01:08:36.420
Her grandma is originally from Hiroshima,

01:08:36.420 --> 01:08:39.660
and she has heard a story from her grandma

01:08:39.660 --> 01:08:44.660
saying that she had a hard
time eating during the war

01:08:45.120 --> 01:08:49.170
and she just had so many struggles

01:08:49.170 --> 01:08:50.790
going through the war period.

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:55.790
So my daughter would like
to know what it was like

01:08:56.017 --> 01:09:01.017
living during that time and
eating during that time.

01:09:01.119 --> 01:09:05.286
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

01:09:13.837 --> 01:09:15.772
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

01:09:15.772 --> 01:09:19.939
(Reiko Tashiro speaking Japanese)

01:09:21.118 --> 01:09:25.368
(Toshiko Tanaka speaking Japanese)

01:09:45.995 --> 01:09:50.995
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

01:10:09.930 --> 01:10:14.930
(Toshiko Tanaka continues
speaking Japanese)

01:10:15.467 --> 01:10:19.320
- What a wonderful question.
- Thank you.

01:10:19.320 --> 01:10:23.760
- She said that there
really was nothing to eat

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:27.060
during the war, and so
people were eating the grass

01:10:27.060 --> 01:10:29.010
from the side of the road.

01:10:29.010 --> 01:10:31.350
And because the grass
from the side of the road

01:10:31.350 --> 01:10:34.890
was irradiated when the bomb was dropped,

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:36.870
lots of people died as a result

01:10:36.870 --> 01:10:41.133
of ingesting the irradiated
weeds on the side of the road.

01:10:42.600 --> 01:10:47.600
Then after the war, food
started to arrive from America

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:51.570
and that included things like soybeans

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:53.940
that had been squeezed of their oil,

01:10:53.940 --> 01:10:57.570
but even eating that
actually tasted wonderful

01:10:57.570 --> 01:11:00.600
after having had no food for so long.

01:11:00.600 --> 01:11:02.550
People were truly starving.

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:06.750
So it's a really wonderful question,

01:11:06.750 --> 01:11:09.783
and thank you for asking.

01:11:13.260 --> 01:11:14.400
Okay, thank you.

01:11:14.400 --> 01:11:16.620
Please join with me for our last time

01:11:16.620 --> 01:11:19.964
in thanking Tanaka-san for
her wonderful, inspiring talk.

01:11:19.964 --> 01:11:23.131
(audience applauding)

01:11:28.688 --> 01:11:32.188
(soft instrumental music)

